Q: How to handle @source for a React UI Library in production? A: <p><code>@source</code> is important for TailwindCSS; however, it has no effect in production because it is not part of the compiled CSS output. It is a CSS-first configuration directive.</p>
<p>When building packages with TailwindCSS, it is not recommended to ship compiled CSS. Doing so negates one of Tailwind’s key advantages: shipping only the styles that are actually used.</p>
<p>TailwindCSS-based packages should provide proper installation instructions so that developers consuming the package can correctly integrate it into their final project. In practice, this means clearly instructing consumers to include an <code>@source</code> directive that points to your package within their project.</p>
<p><strong>final project CSS entrypoint</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;

@source &quot;./path/to/project-root/node_modules/your-library-name/components&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<p><strong>final project CSS entrypoint</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;

@import &quot;your-library-name/main.css&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>your library packages.json</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;exports&quot;: {
    &quot;./main.css&quot;: &quot;./main.css&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>your library main.css</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@source &quot;./components&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: <em>The advantage of <code>main.css</code> is that you can also ship custom <code>@theme</code>, <code>@utility</code>, and <code>@custom-variant</code> definitions.</em></p>
<p>However, applying this naively can introduce inefficiencies. A global <code>@source</code> reference to your entire UI library will cause Tailwind to generate styles for every file in the library, regardless of whether those components are actually used. Therefore, instead of recommending a single global <code>@source</code>, it is preferable for the consuming developer to include component-specific <code>@source</code> directives only for the components they use.</p>
<p><strong>final project CSS entrypoint</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;

@source &quot;./path/to/project-root/node_modules/your-library-name/components/Input.tsx&quot;;
@source &quot;./path/to/project-root/node_modules/your-library-name/components/Calendar.tsx&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>You can simplify this by providing a dedicated CSS file for each component in your UI library, where each CSS file internally references its corresponding component via a relative <code>@source</code>. In that case, the consuming project does not need to define <code>@source</code> manually; it is sufficient to import the CSS files of the components that are actually used.</p>
<p><strong>final project CSS entrypoint</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;

@import &quot;your-library-name/input.css&quot;;
@import &quot;your-library-name/calendar.css&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>your library packages.json</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;exports&quot;: {
    &quot;./input.css&quot;: &quot;./input.css&quot;,
    &quot;./calendar.css&quot;: &quot;./calendar.css&quot;,
    &quot;./button.css&quot;: &quot;./button.css&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>your library input.css</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@source &quot;./components/Input.tsx&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>your library calendar.css</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@source &quot;./components/Calendar.tsx&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>References:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/79799492/15167500">All components from the UI package are included in the project's generated CSS, but I don't use every component</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79433685/15167500">TailwindCSS does not discover class names used in the NPM package</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/79820754/15167500">Shipping a UI package alongside projects in a monorepo</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79416262/19655631">How to enable Tailwind CSS v4.0 for the packages/ui components in Turborepo?</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Independently of this, you can still have a <code>main.css</code>, for example to provide a custom <code>@theme</code> color. Moreover, the color can remain overridable at the entry point.</p>
<p><strong>final project CSS entrypoint</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;

@import &quot;your-library-name/main.css&quot;;
@import &quot;your-library-name/input.css&quot;;
@import &quot;your-library-name/calendar.css&quot;;

/*
@theme {
  --color-ui: #555;
}
*/
</code></pre>
<p><strong>your library packages.json</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;exports&quot;: {
    &quot;./main.css&quot;: &quot;./main.css&quot;,
    &quot;./input.css&quot;: &quot;./input.css&quot;,
    &quot;./calendar.css&quot;: &quot;./calendar.css&quot;,
    &quot;./button.css&quot;: &quot;./button.css&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>your library main.css</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@theme {
  --color-ui: #000;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>your library input.css</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@source &quot;./components/Input.tsx&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>your library calendar.css</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@source &quot;./components/Calendar.tsx&quot;;
</code></pre>

Q: Best practice for ValueState access: multiple value() calls vs caching in instance variable? A: <p>I don't think you want to jump directly into implementing Option 1 or Option 2 completely as written, but both have merit and there's likely a balance to strike between them:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Option 1</strong> - This option is calling <code>value()</code> multiple times each time a record is processed, which can be expensive. (To answer your question, generally yes: it's going to result in a read-deserialization from your backend.) What you're going to want to do is try to adopt a pattern of reading the value once, reuse locally, and ultimately batch any changes in a single <code>update()</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Option 2</strong> - Keeping a long-lived cache copy of your value for an extended period of time can be <em>dangerous</em> if you aren't careful about managing it. You'd need to continually refresh it for each subsequent record, which could get expensive on its own.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Ideally, you want to read your state once per record, store it locally/operate on it, and then write it back once. This might look something like updating your existing manager to only act on the <em>value</em> (e.g. business logic) and not the state store itself, and similarly only reading/writing once within your actual process function.</p>
<p>To do this, we could update your <code>DeviceStateManager</code> to no longer reference the state reference itself but instead act only on the value (e.g. <code>DeviceData</code>):</p>
<pre><code>public final class DeviceStateManager {

  public DeviceData updateMetrics(DeviceData data, Measurement measurement) {
    if (data == null) {
      return null;
    }
    data.addMeasurement(measurement);
    return data;
  }

  public boolean isAnomalyDetected(DeviceData data, double threshold) {
    return data != null &amp;&amp; data.getAverage() &gt; threshold;
  }

  public Alert generateAlert(DeviceData data) {
    if (data == null) {
      return null;
    }
    return new Alert(
        data.getDeviceId(),
        data.getLastValue(),
        data.getTimestamp()
    );
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Similarly in your function, you'd focus on reading the value once, performing your business logic, and update it accordingly:</p>
<pre><code>public class IoTProcessFunction extends KeyedProcessFunction&lt;String, Event, Alert&gt; {

  private transient ValueState&lt;DeviceData&gt; deviceState;
  private transient DeviceStateManager stateManager;

  @Override
  public void open(Configuration parameters) {
    ValueStateDescriptor&lt;DeviceData&gt; desc =
        new ValueStateDescriptor&lt;&gt;(&quot;device-state&quot;, DeviceData.class);
    deviceState = getRuntimeContext().getState(desc);
    // IMPORTANT - you don't want to initialize this in every processElement call
    // as it introduces a ton of overhead (this is what open() is for)
    stateManager = new DeviceStateManager();
  }

  @Override
  public void processElement(Event event, Context ctx, Collector&lt;Alert&gt; out) throws Exception {
    // Read/deserialize the data into a variable
    DeviceData data = deviceState.value(); 

    // Do your thing
    data = stateManager.updateMetrics(data, event.getMeasurement());

    // Handle the write only if you need to do so (other business logic?)
    if (data != null) {
      // Write only once if necessary similar to the read)
      deviceState.update(data);

      // Notice this is acting on your value, not the state store so no additional
      // read/write
      if (stateManager.isAnomalyDetected(data, 100.0)) {
        Alert alert = stateManager.generateAlert(data);
        if (alert != null) {
          out.collect(alert);
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>There's obviously tons of ways to slice this, depending on how much you want to use the external manager object, but this is likely a pretty simple improvement.</p>
<p>As far as your questions go, as I may not have directly answered all of them:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Does <code>ValueState.value()</code> perform deserialization on each call, or is the value already cached internally by Flink's state backend?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Each call to <code>value()</code> is going to result in a read and deserialization from the state backend, at least with RocksDB. RocksDB will typically do some caching of its own, but it's not going to be caching your object (at least not in the way you would in say, a hashmap).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In Option 1, I'm calling <code>deviceState.value()</code> multiple times (in <code>isAnomalyDetected()</code> it's called twice, in <code>generateAlert()</code> four times). Is there a performance penalty?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yup, as mentioned previously, calling <code>value()</code> generally has read and serialization overhead and this can add up.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Is Option 2 duplicating Flink’s internal caching?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So RocksDB will do some caching, although not as efficient/the same as caching the deserialized copy. Caching post-deserialization <em>is</em> going to be highly performant, but as I mentioned earlier, you'd be trading off that performance for ensuring the complexity, which isn't always worth the risk.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What's the recommended best practice for high-throughput scenarios?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It depends, as there's always a lot of knobs to turn. But you can't really go wrong with limiting the number of state read-deserialzation operations (e.g., read state once per record, operate on it locally, and write it back once).</p>
